.env
.env.*
!.env.example
node_modules/
results/
*.mp4
*.nut
*.webm
# 🔴 EXCEPT THE ONE FILM THE SITE ACTUALLY SERVES. `*.mp4` is right for the
# recordings and captures this repo generates, and it silently excluded a
# deliberate ASSET: `/stage/`'s background reached the deployed edge and would
# have been absent from every clone, because `build.mjs` copies the WORKING TREE
# rather than the index. An asset live on the edge and missing from the repo is
# the kind of gap nobody notices until somebody clones it.
!demo/resources/*.mp4
src/.last-input
auto.crt
auto.key
rig/moq/moq-rs/
rig/whep/live_input.json
rig/whep/logs/
rig/moq/moq-rs-draft14/
rig/moq/spike/logs/
*-udd/
__pycache__/
*.log
.wrangler/
proto/megatimeline/*-cache.jsonl
proto/deck/.chrome-verify/
proto/loops/.chrome-verify/

# headless Chrome profiles (verify rigs) — never artifacts
**/.chrome-verify/
**/.chrome-remote/

# studio run logs: transient, and the run artifacts under studio/runs are the record
studio/logs/

**/.chrome-mobile/

# workers/pub pulls @cloudflare/containers
workers/pub/node_modules/

# Google service-account keys. A private key, not a config file — anyone holding
# one can push to every device of the project it belongs to, and there is no
# password to change afterwards, only a revocation in the console.
#
# ⚠️ THESE PATTERNS ARE A SECOND LINE, NOT THE FIRST. The first is: never put one
# under `demo/`, which `workers/view/build.mjs` ENUMERATES and uploads publicly.
# The repo root is fenced off from that enumerator on purpose — build.mjs:249,
# "because the REPO ROOT holds .env" — which is why a key at the root is safe
# from the deploy and only needs protecting from git.
*serviceAccount*.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
# 🔴 AND THE NAME SOMEBODY ACTUALLY CHOSE. The two patterns above were written
# from the name Firebase's console DOWNLOADS, and the key was then saved as
# `positron-push-sa.json` — which matches neither. MEASURED: `git check-ignore`
# said nothing and `git status` listed it as untracked, one `git add` from being
# committed. A pattern that only covers the default name protects the one case
# nobody was going to get wrong.
*-sa.json
*-sa.*.json
positron-push-sa.json
.tap/

# ── purchased content, which is not ours to publish ────────────────────────
# 🔴 A PAID SOUNDBANK LIVES HERE AND MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED. Said plainly
# 2026-09-21: *"i purchased them. do not git them"*. The zip arrived untracked
# and UNIGNORED, so a single careless `git add -A` would have published
# somebody's commercial patch library from a repository that is pushed to
# GitHub. The hook in `.claude/hooks/` refuses `git add -A` here, but a hook is
# a second line and this is the first.
# ⚠️ `New Pack.circuitpack` in the root is DIFFERENT and is deliberately
# tracked: it is the owner's own Circuit backup and CLAUDE.md records why it
# must not be deleted. Ours to keep, theirs to sell.
# 🔴 EVERYTHING IN `purchased/` IS IGNORED EXCEPT ITS README, which is tracked
# ON PURPOSE so a fresh clone can see why the directory is empty. An ignored
# directory with nothing in it does not exist in git at all, and the next person
# would make a second place for the same thing.
purchased/*
!purchased/README.md
# ⚠️ AND NOT A RULE PER PURCHASE. The first draft of this also listed
# `Synth-Patches.com*`, `*.circuittrackspack` and `Soundbank*/`, which is a
# filename rule that rots the moment the next thing arrives under a different
# name, and which quietly teaches the next person that a download may sit in
# the root as long as somebody remembers to add a line. One directory, one
# rule, and the rule is about WHERE a thing lives rather than what it is called.
